sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/grn-memory-generic-or-biological--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Attribution by surgery: rewire topology vs shuffle parameters in real models" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/grn-memory-generic-or-biological> ;
    dct:description "Random networks differ from biological ones in many ways at once. A sharper design perturbs the biological models themselves along one axis at a time. For each of the 35 models: (a) topology surgery, degree-preserving edge swaps (100 swaps per edge) keeping every reaction's rate law and parameters; (b) parameter surgery, permuting parameter values among reactions of the same kinetic type, keeping topology; (c) kinetic surgery, replacing Hill terms with Michaelis–Menten terms (n = 1), keeping topology and parameters; (d) a combination. Generate 20 variants per model per surgery, discard variants without a stable steady state, and re-run the memory protocol. Memory that survives (a) but not (b) points to tuned parameters; memory surviving (b) but lost under (c) points to cooperativity (ultrasensitivity) as the generic cause. This decomposes any biological enrichment from approach 1 into causes." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Retention rates of each memory type under topology, parameter and kinetic surgery. For example, '80% of UCS memory survives topology rewiring but only 20% survives replacing Hill terms' would identify ultrasensitivity, not network wiring, as the cause." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Most surgical variants lose steady-state stability, so the surviving variants are a biased subset and retention rates are uninterpretable." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement SBML-level surgery operators with libsbml; verify that variants remain valid and stable. 2. Pre-register the attribution logic (which surgery outcomes imply which cause). 3. Run the memory protocol on all variants. 4. Report per-memory-type retention rates under each surgery with CIs." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: ode-simulation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: biomodels" ;
    skos:note "Tool: random-network-nulls" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-grn-learning-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-grn-learning-08> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-grn-learning-11> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-grn-learning-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-grn-learning-16> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biomodels/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2021.102131> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/grn-memory-generic-or-biological> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is memory in gene regulatory network models generic or a biological signature?" .

  <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biomodels/> dct:title "BioModels" ;
    dct:description "Source SBML models for surgery" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2021.102131> dct:title "Gene regulatory networks exhibit several kinds of memory: Quantification of memory in biological and random transcriptional networks" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Biswas et al. 2021" .
}

